**Yes.** The answer is yes: absolute summability of the double series guarantees that the two iterated sums are equal.

---

**Proof.**

Assume $\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}\sum_{j=1}^{\infty}|a_{ij}|<\infty$.

**Step 1 (Row sums exist and are absolutely summable).** For each fixed $i$,
$$\sum_{j=1}^{\infty}|a_{ij}|\le \sum_{i=1}^{\infty}\sum_{j=1}^{\infty}|a_{ij}|<\infty,$$
so $R_i:=\sum_{j=1}^{\infty}a_{ij}$ converges absolutely, hence converges. Moreover
$$\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}|R_i|\le\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}\sum_{j=1}^{\infty}|a_{ij}|<\infty,$$
so $\sum_i R_i$ converges absolutely.

**Step 2 (Column sums exist and are absolutely summable).** By the identical argument with the roles of $i$ and $j$ exchanged, each $C_j:=\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}a_{ij}$ converges, and $\sum_j|C_j|<\infty$, so $\sum_j C_j$ converges absolutely.

**Step 3 (Rectangular partial sums converge jointly to $\sum_i R_i$).** Let $S_{M,N}:=\sum_{i=1}^{M}\sum_{j=1}^{N}a_{ij}$. Then
$$\Bigl|S_{M,N}-\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}R_i\Bigr|\le\sum_{i=1}^{M}\sum_{j=N+1}^{\infty}|a_{ij}|+\sum_{i=M+1}^{\infty}|R_i|.$$

For the first term: since $\sum_{j=N+1}^{\infty}|a_{ij}|\le\sum_{j=1}^{\infty}|a_{ij}|$ and $\sum_i\sum_j|a_{ij}|<\infty$, the Dominated Convergence Theorem (applied to the counting measure on $\mathbb{N}$) gives
$$\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}\sum_{j=N+1}^{\infty}|a_{ij}|\xrightarrow[N\to\infty]{}0.$$

For the second term: $\sum_{i=M+1}^{\infty}|R_i|\to 0$ as $M\to\infty$ since $\sum_i|R_i|<\infty$.

Hence for every $\varepsilon>0$ we can choose $M$ so that $\sum_{i=M+1}^{\infty}|R_i|<\varepsilon/2$, and then $N$ so that $\sum_{i=1}^{M}\sum_{j=N+1}^{\infty}|a_{ij}|<\varepsilon/2$, yielding $|S_{M,N}-\sum_i R_i|<\varepsilon$. Thus
$$S_{M,N}\xrightarrow[M,N\to\infty]{}\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}R_i=\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}\sum_{j=1}^{\infty}a_{ij}.$$

**Step 4 (Same joint limit equals $\sum_j C_j$).** Repeating Step 3 with rows and columns interchanged gives
$$S_{M,N}\xrightarrow[M,N\to\infty]{}\sum_{j=1}^{\infty}C_j=\sum_{j=1}^{\infty}\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}a_{ij}.$$

Since the joint limit is unique,
$$\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}\sum_{j=1}^{\infty}a_{ij}=\sum_{j=1}^{\infty}\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}a_{ij}.\qquad\blacksquare$$

